<p>Arab's location on Brindlee Mountain creates drainage challenges that flat-land homeowners don't face. When 55+ inches of rain falls each year on hilly terrain, water picks up speed as it flows downhill — roughly 1.5x faster on a 10% grade. If your gutters can't capture and redirect that water, it ends up exactly where you don't want it — at your foundation, moving faster than it would on flat ground.</p>

<p>Many older <a href="/recent-projects/">Arab homes</a> were built with sectional gutters that have been leaking for years. The seams have separated, debris accumulates at every joint, and water spills over during moderate rain. We've seen homes where decades of improper drainage have caused foundation cracks, basement moisture, and erosion costing $10,000-30,000 to repair.</p>

<p>Even newer construction in Arab often has undersized or poorly installed gutters. Builders focus on getting homes up quickly and cheaply, not on long-term drainage performance. The result: 5-inch gutters that overflow, 2x3 downspouts that back up, and drainage systems that fail exactly when you need them most — during heavy mountain storms.</p>

<p><strong>Our approach:</strong> We size gutters for mountain conditions, not minimum code. That means 6-inch troughs, 3x4 downspouts, and strategic positioning that accounts for your lot's actual slope — not cookie-cutter installation.</p>
